  <Groups>
    <Group elementId="CPLX-2783" textLabel="CPLX-2783" type="Complex">
      <Xref identifier="CPLX-2783" dataSource="plantcyc" />
      <Graphics centerX="600" centerY="335" width="140" height="100" textColor="666666" fontName="Arial" fontSize="10" borderColor="9900CC" borderStyle="Dashed" borderWidth="2.0" fillColor="F5E6FF" shapeType="Rectangle" zOrder="-1" />
      <Comment>Plant 4- hydroxyphenylpyruvate dioxygenase (HPPD) has been genetically and biochemically demonstrated involved in tocopherol and plastoquinone biosynthesis. In mammals and non-photosynthetic bacteria, however, HPPD is involved in tyrosine degradation. Plant HPPD may also be involved in tyrosine degradation. HPPD has been purified from Arabidopsis and carrot, and it appears as a homodimer in both species. However, maize HPPD was reported a monomer. Unlike all other enzymes of the tocopherol biosynthesis pathway, which are localized in the chloroplast, the Arabidopsis and carrot HPPDs are both shown localized in the cytosol. HPPD is encoded by a single copy gene in Arabidopsis.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="CPLX-2783" />
